import RequestProject.Epistemic.Commitment

/-!
# Correlated evidence is not independent confirmation

Several references can be derived from a single source episode: an import from
one database, one press release republished, one survey re-tabulated.  Counting
them as separate confirmations is a mistake that the arithmetic of a tally makes
easy, so this module keeps the two counts apart.

* `independentSupport` counts *roots*, not items, and is never more than the
  number of items (`independentSupport_le_length`); it collapses when the items
  share a root (`independentSupport_shared_root`).
* Naive counting therefore overstates support, and the overstatement can decide
  an action: at threshold 2, counting items commits while counting sources does
  not (`naive_counting_commits_independent_does_not`).
* Independence is exactly distinctness of roots
  (`provenanceIndependent_iff_root_ne`), so a claim's second citation to the
  same import is not evidence (`same_root_not_independent`).

The second half is the obligation that comes with dependence.  If a derived
item depends on a source, a change at the source *reopens* it, reopening is
transitive along dependency paths (`reopens_trans`), and the set of items a
change reopens is exactly the transitive closure of the dependency relation
(`reopens_iff_transGen`).  Independence is what stops that closure from
spreading: nothing reopens an item whose root did not change
(`not_reopens_of_independent`).
-/

